Sculpt and rig the **Sunfront Solar Extractor**, a sunlight harvester — a fanned rank of
angled solar-collector panels splayed off a canted spine on a low faceted pedestal,
drinking the sun into a glowing stored-sol core — as a {{voxel.width}}x{{voxel.height}}x{{voxel.depth}} opaque-voxel model using
only the `voxel-anim` tool. You build it by **painting discrete opaque cells** and you
**author its panel-tracking, collector-bloom, and core-charge animations** as F-curves.
It is an economy structure: it produces **no units** and has no combat role — it just
harvests light, so it must never read as a factory.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief tells
you *what the Extractor is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build, how you
break the harvester into a fixed body and its moving pieces, where you place the joints,
and how you shape every form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a
self-cycling sunlight harvester needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on
your `PATH`. You build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**, and the
ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to `parts/<part>.actions.json` —
together with the rig you define are your submission.

You paint the geometry as **discrete opaque cells**: `voxel-anim` sets and clears single
voxels, fills and strokes boxes, draws 3D lines and spheres, and mirrors across a plane
— each cell an opaque `#rrggbb` color; there is **no transparency**. Every voxel
operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`; each part is sculpted **separately**
in its own preview and log, in the shared volume's coordinates. The volume starts
**empty**.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving each a
parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and author the required
animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Every voxel operation targets a part
with `--part <name>`, so define a part before you sculpt into it. Each call **records
only** — it appends the operation to that part's log and renders nothing; run
`voxel-anim render` when you want to (re)draw the per-part previews (`parts/<part>.png`)
and the assembled scene.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name pedestal
voxel-anim fill-box --part pedestal --x0 1 --y0 0 --z0 1 --x1 5 --y1 5 --z1 5 --color "#c69a4b"
voxel-anim add-sphere --part sol_core --cx 3 --cy 3 --cz 3 --r 2 --color "#ff9d2e"
```

(The part names above are just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`voxel-anim --help` to list every voxel operation **and** the rig and animation
subcommands (defining parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and authoring animations
with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`), and `voxel-anim <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Run
`voxel-anim render`, then read `parts/<part>.png`** to see that part's progress **and
the assembled-scene previews under `scene/` (`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`,
`scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole harvester composed from all your parts,
drawn when you run `voxel-anim render`. The scene views are how you confirm the parts
actually fit together: that the fan splays across the spine, the spine rises from the
pedestal, and the core sits housed at its heart. Because each part is sculpted in the
shared volume's coordinates, position each part where it belongs on the finished
harvester; the scene simply composes them there.

