## What to deliver

Build and rig the harvester up operation by operation until each `parts/<part>.png`
matches the brief, the assembled `scene/` previews show the parts fitting together
correctly, and the rig is complete: a low faceted pedestal, a canted spine rising off
it, a wide fan of angled solar-collector panels splayed across the top, and a glowing
stored-sol core housed at its heart. **Author the three required animations** as F-curves
with the `voxel-anim` animation subcommands: `panel_track` (a slow, eased sweep of the
whole fan across the sky to follow the sun and back), `collector_bloom` (a breathing
spread-and-close of the splayed panels), and `sol_charge` (a slow breathing
brighten-and-swell of the core as it banks light) — all three self-playing, so the
harvester cycles on its own while the pedestal stays fixed. None of them produces or
releases a unit. The animations are the fixed contract — produce all three, by those
names; the parts and joints that realize them are yours. Before you finish, run
`voxel-anim render` so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry your result is built from —
an unrendered part is scored as empty (`voxel-anim render --component <part>` redraws one
part; `voxel-anim render --time <ms> --animation <name>` renders the model posed at that
instant of an animation to check the motion). When you are satisfied, stop: the recorded
per-part logs and `rig.json` are your finished submission.
